Simple Mac-side command list: resource group → AKS → Helm Keycloak in `start-dev` → `kubectl port-forward` to `localhost`.

Swap names/region as needed. Use a small cluster; this is for desktop testing only.

```bash
# --- 0. Login / context ---
az login
az account set --subscription "<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>"

# Optional: install kubectl + helm if missing
az aks install-cli
brew install helm

# --- 1. Variables ---
export RG="rg-aks-keycloak-dev"
export LOC="eastus"
export AKS="aks-keycloak-dev"
export NS="keycloak"

# --- 2. Resource group ---
az group create --name $RG --location $LOC

# --- 3. AKS (1 node, cheap, SSH keys generated) ---
az aks create \
  --resource-group $RG \
  --name $AKS \
  --node-count 1 \
  --node-vm-size Standard_B2s \
  --generate-ssh-keys \
  --enable-managed-identity

# Wait until Succeeded
az aks show -g $RG -n $AKS --query provisioningState -o tsv

# --- 4. kubeconfig on the Mac ---
az aks get-credentials --resource-group $RG --name $AKS --overwrite-existing
kubectl get nodes

# --- 5. Namespace ---
kubectl create namespace $NS

# --- 6. Helm repo (codecentric keycloakx = official Quay image) ---
helm repo add codecentric https://codecentric.github.io/helm-charts
helm repo update

# --- 7. Install Keycloak in start-dev ---
# H2 in-pod DB, HTTP on 8080, hostname checks off so localhost works
helm upgrade --install keycloak codecentric/keycloakx \
  --namespace $NS \
  --set command="{start-dev}" \
  --set extraEnv[0].name=KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USERNAME \
  --set extraEnv[0].value=admin \
  --set extraEnv[1].name=KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD \
  --set extraEnv[1].value=admin \
  --set extraEnv[2].name=KC_HTTP_ENABLED \
  --set extraEnv[2].value="true" \
  --set extraEnv[3].name=KC_HOSTNAME_STRICT \
  --set extraEnv[3].value="false" \
  --set extraEnv[4].name=KC_HOSTNAME \
  --set extraEnv[4].value=localhost \
  --set extraEnv[5].name=KC_HOSTNAME_PORT \
  --set extraEnv[5].value="8080" \
  --set service.type=ClusterIP \
  --set http.httpEnabled=true

# Wait for the pod
kubectl -n $NS rollout status statefulset/keycloak --timeout=180s
kubectl -n $NS get pods,svc

# --- 8. Localhost from the Mac ---
# Leave this terminal open
kubectl -n $NS port-forward svc/keycloak-keycloakx-http 8080:8080
```

Open:

- Admin console: http://localhost:8080  
- Login: `admin` / `admin`

If the service name differs, list it and forward that:

```bash
kubectl -n $NS get svc
kubectl -n $NS port-forward svc/<SERVICE_NAME> 8080:8080
```

### Cleanup

```bash
helm -n $NS uninstall keycloak
az aks delete -g $RG -n $AKS --yes --no-wait
az group delete -n $RG --yes --no-wait
```

Notes:

- `start-dev` is for local/dev only (HTTP, H2, no HA). Do not use this as a production SSO.
- Port-forward is the right way to hit Keycloak from a Mac without a public LoadBalancer or Ingress.
- If the admin UI redirects oddly, keep `KC_HOSTNAME=localhost` and `KC_HOSTNAME_PORT=8080` as above, and use exactly `http://localhost:8080`.
- AKS create takes several minutes; the Helm install is usually ready in 1–2 minutes after the cluster is up.